# Documentation Standards

## Source of truth

- Canonical documentation lives in `docs/` (not in `.cursor/rules/`).
- The **authoritative docs-sync / definition-of-done rule** is `09-docs-auto-update.mdc`.

## Documentation map (single source of truth)

Keep documentation in **one canonical location** per topic. Prefer updating an existing doc over creating a new one.

- `docs/00_index/`
  - `README.md`: main entry point
  - `CURSOR_INDEX.md`: quick navigation for AI + humans
  - `MASTER_PLAN.md`, `MIGRATION_MAP.md`: planning
  - `DECISIONS.md`: ADR-lite decision log
  - `KNOWN_INCONSISTENCIES.md`: doc/code mismatches to resolve

- `docs/10_dev/`
  - build/how-to, standards, contributing, status, roadmap

- `docs/20_arch/`
  - architecture and design explanations

- `docs/30_protocols/<proto>/`
  - protocol specs, compatibility notes, packet layouts, diagnostics, test fixtures

- `docs/40_quality/`
  - security, performance, testing schedules, audits

- `docs/50_user/`
  - end-user guides, settings reference

Also keep the small “moved” pointer files at `docs/STATUS.md`, `docs/ROADMAP.md`, etc. aligned with the canonical targets.

## Create vs update

Before creating any new doc:
1. Search the repository for an existing doc to extend (`rg -n "<keyword>" docs/`).
2. If an existing doc covers 70%+ of the topic, **update it**.
3. If a new doc is truly required, place it in the correct folder and add it to:
   - `docs/00_index/CURSOR_INDEX.md` (if it’s a primary entry point)
   - `docs/00_index/README.md` if it changes the overall navigation

Avoid duplicates like multiple “status” or “roadmap” docs with different truth.

## Required header for developer docs

For developer-facing docs, include a metadata block at the top (keep it current):

```markdown
Status: draft|active|deprecated
Last updated: YYYY-MM-DD
Scope: <one sentence>
Source of truth: <code paths / spec links>
```

Update “Last updated” whenever substantive content changes.

## Writing conventions

- **English only** for developer docs, comments, and commit messages.
- Prefer Markdown. Avoid heavy HTML; keep docs readable in plain text.
- Prefer short sections and lists; avoid “wall of text”.
- Always link claims to:
  - code paths (file/function names), and/or
  - protocol specs, and/or
  - test fixtures / reproduction steps.

## Code and comment documentation (in code)

- Comments must be in English.
- For public-facing classes/functions, prefer Doxygen-style blocks and document:
  - purpose/behavior
  - parameters and return values
  - ownership/lifetime and thread-safety notes when relevant
- Avoid comment drift: if behavior changes, update or remove the outdated comment.

## Avoid

- Outdated comments that don't match code
- Obvious comments that just repeat the code
- Missing documentation for public APIs
- Not documenting thread safety requirements
- Not documenting ownership semantics
- Comments in languages other than English
